ipped your response  team the game swaps into a turn-based  strategy game this is where the bulk of  your time with ex-con will be spent the  game is turn-based meaning that you and  the aliens take turns in moving and  these movements are limited by a set  number of action points every action you  take costs points moving shooting  reloading crouching even priming  grenades so you have to be careful to  ensure you're spending your AP wisely it  plays like a game of chess where every  move is as much about creating offensive  opportunities as it is ensuring your  pieces are safe from opposition attack  once you've made your moves to game  swaps control to the aliens and you have  to watch is the aliens Scott are about  in the darkness emerging to take pop  shots at your fragile troopers and  reacting to your tactics  XCOM was also incredibly atmospheric  distant alien noises and human screams  coupled with creepy music and the  ominous fog of war meant that even  stepping out of your ship required  bravery soldiers could be killed in an  instant doing so often caused new  recruits to freak the hell out and flee  and shoot wildly tactical advantages  could be flipped in an instant meaning  you never really felt like you were in  full control  I asked Jake what it was about the first  game that made it so memorable um so the  original XCOM I got that when I was a  senior in high school I played them  obsessively and if you play that you  know I have so many memories of sitting  there alone at night it's very spooky  game you know and so you just I just  have many of these memories just me  sitting for the computer Greg and I my  art director we went and looked back at  it recently and it's you know it's like  340 by 260 or 3 and you're like oh how  did that work like how was I so  terrified but you've read so much into  you know it was very hard to understand  like and so it was like you didn't know  why things were having it felt like a  real simulation I think that'