ter one the game was  really difficult terribly real difficult  but it was interesting how once it went  under the water and all these crazy  aliens then I just didn't feel a strong  connection to the places and so an  apocalypse was awesome you know it was  awesome but it was it looks really  sci-fi that gradualism while terror from  the deep was being made out of house  mythos were working on the third game  apocalypse this was set a century after  the end of the second game as humanity  has been forced to settle into mega  cities to escape the global meltdown of  the planets ecosystem released in 1997  it was quite a departure from the main  series featuring new graphics interface  and adoptive enemy AI and even allows  you to play the game in real time or in  the traditional turn-based mode after  apocalypse is released however mythos  were worried that they'd lose the  lawsuit if they tried to retain the IP  in court so they struck a licensing deal  for apocalypse and handed over the XCOM  license to microprose if you talk about  any other's past that I don't have that  much experience I'm an interceptor  enforcer yeah I don't I suppose I should  explain you were so close to being  complete and so you've yet to undergo a  full subsystem stress test I see jake  draws a blank here for a good reason the  next decade of ex-con games has almost  been lost to history but it contains a  bunch of games terrible review scores  and an IP being fenced from company to  company the first game XCOM interceptor  made by microprose now that they own the  IP was released in 1998 it was a flight  sim mixed with the usual base management  stuff set before apocalypse it had a  completely different tone to the  original three games but loads of  bizarre pop-culture references and full  of the type of humor  you're embarrassing uncle would make  over Thanksgiving dinner after the  release of interceptor Hasbro the toy  makers bought the struggling Microprose  in turn taking over the XCOM brand with  an eye to assimilating it into its  various toy ranges Hasbro Interactive's  first attempt at this was 1999  XCOM first alien invasion a play by male  multiplayer game now if you've never  heard of play by male multiplayer it's  essentially the email equivalent of  playing a game of chess over the mail if  that wasn't bad enough Hasbro wasn't  done yet soon afterwards the struggling  company canceled two XCOM games they had  adopted in the microprose takeover  Alliance a first-person shooter and  Genesis a strategy game set in a world  familiar to fans of the original game  neither was close enough to complete and  financial trouble method Hasbro  to sell something quick so they took  assets from both games and then cobbled  them together to make 2001 XCOM enforcer  a by-the-numbers third-person shooter  would barely a thematic or tonal link to  the original games I played a bunch of  it a couple of years ago and it's mad  just take a look yourself the alien  invaders draw nearer with every  nanosecond  bleating enforcer finish charging up get  out there and do what you were made to  do  eradicate don't alien soon afterwards  the assets of Hasbro interactive were  sold to French developer Infogrames an  ex-con became stuck in a game of IP  tennis for a couple of years in 2004  Infogrames now rebranded Atari sold the  rights to the civilization franchise to  take to interactive the struggling  company then sold back the digital  rights to a bunch of Hasbro properties  the following year transformers in My  Little Pony and that same year in June  of 2005 take-two interactive bought a  bunch more IPS from Atari one of them  being XCOM take two went right to work  on their IP in April 2010 2k Marin  developers of Bioshock - an answer they  were working on a new XCOM and a month  later at e3 we saw the game entitled  XCOM was a first-person shooter set in  the 50s where at least according to the  trailer use the variety weapons to kill  a bunch of weird black goo sadly for 2k  the trailer was not well received by  fans of the series who are pining for a  classic XCOM game but little did they  know one was already in development you  see in 2005 the same year take to it  acquired the ex-con license a year after  they had acquired the civilizations  license it also acquired fair access  games the studio set up by former  Microprose developers Sid Meier Jeff  Briggs and Brian Reynolds who had left  the company to release some of the best  strategy games of all time  Alpha Centauri and civilization the  stage was set for the ultimate comeback  while 2k Marines XCOM game would change  into a third-person shooter the Bureau  XCOM declassified came out in August  2013  behind closed doors the keys to a  classic XCOM were being handed to a  young designer and a lifelong found the  series his name is Jake I'm going to  talk to him about it tomorrow and that's  where we leave today's story make sure  you check back tomorrow to see how Jake  and his team at Firaxis took a series  that have been kicked from pillar to  post and gave it the rebirth it deserves  see you that commander  you

